.TH NEUTRINO 1 "November 2026" "neutrino 2.34.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
neutrino \- a small functional array language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B neutrino
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file.nu ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B neutrino
is a small functional array language with Octave-flavoured syntax,
implemented in C23. It has a strict numeric tower (Int exact until
overflow, then Float), first-class functions, immutable refcounted
values, complex numbers, and a linear algebra core: matrix multiply,
left/right division, least squares, det/inv, and lu/qr/chol/eig/svd
decompositions, with real fast paths for real inputs.
.PP
With no arguments it starts the REPL: line editing, history, tab
completion, and
.B ans
(the last value you saw and didn't name). With a
.I file.nu
argument it executes the script and exits; a
.B #!/usr/bin/env neutrino
shebang line works.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
print version and build date, then exit
.TP
.B \-\-tokens
dump the token stream of the input instead of evaluating
.TP
.B \-\-ast
dump the parse tree instead of evaluating
.TP
.B \-\-dis
disassemble the compiled bytecode instead of evaluating
.TP
.B \-\-sample
print a small sample program
.SH "REPL COMMANDS"
Typed bare at the prompt (not as function calls):
.B help
lists every builtin and
.B help(name)
shows one with verified examples;
.B who
lists the workspace, with loaded packages folded into one-line shelves
.RB ( who(\[dq]finance\[dq])
opens one);
.B packeur
opens a package picker \(em arrows or j/k move, space toggles, Enter
loads newly checked packages and clears newly unchecked shelves;
.B clear()
empties the workspace and
.B clear(\[dq]name\[dq])
removes a variable or a whole package shelf;
.B manual
pages the rendered manual
.RB ( manual\ packages ,
.BR manual\ changelog ,
\&...);
.B format(n)
sets displayed significant digits and
.B format(\[dq]fixed\[dq],\ d)
sets decimals;
.B mem
shows workspace size;
.B dis(f)
disassembles a function;
.B !cmd
runs a shell command.
.SH PACKAGES
The standard library ships as
.I .nu
packages (probability distributions, polynomials, finance, physical
constants, symbolic differentiation, and more).
.B load(\[dq]dist\[dq])
loads one by bare name;
.B ls(\[dq]packages\[dq])
lists them. Bare names are searched, in order: the literal path;
.I packages/NAME.nu
in the current directory; each colon-separated directory in
.BR $NEUTRINO_PATH ;
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/neutrino/packages
(default
.IR ~/.local/share/neutrino/packages ,
where
.B make install
puts them); and finally
.I packages/
beside the real binary, symlinks resolved.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NEUTRINO_PATH
colon-separated extra directories searched by
.BR load .
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
overrides the user data directory root.
.TP
.B NEUTRINO_PLOT_TERM
set to
.B ascii
to force terminal plots.
.TP
.B PAGER
pager for long output and
.BR manual .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.neutrino_history
REPL history.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/neutrino/packages/
installed standard packages.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ neutrino
neutrino> load("dist"); norm.cdf(1.96, 0, 1)
0.975002
neutrino> [1,2,3,4] |> map((_ .^ 2), @) |> sum(@)
30
$ echo 'prod[k = 1:20] k' | neutrino
2432902008176640000
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The full manual (MANUAL.pdf), the packages guide (PACKAGES.pdf), and
the problem book (BOOK.pdf) ship with the source and render in the
REPL via
.BR manual .
Browser build and documentation:
.UR https://micomrkaic.github.io/Neutrino/
